  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a droplet or bubble behaves on a solid surface through three broad stages: wetting, growth, and departure, with special emphasis on the role of surface roughness and contact-line physics. In the wetting stage, the sketches show a liquid body resting on a surface with a defined contact angle and then illustrate contact-angle hysteresis, where advancing and receding angles differ as motion begins. Rough surfaces are shown to support two classic wetting states: Wenzel wetting, where the fluid fills the texture, and Cassie wetting, where it sits partly above the roughness.\n\nDuring growth, the droplet or bubble enlarges either by absorbing material from the surrounding phase or by coalescing with neighbors. On textured surfaces, growth can occur within the roughness, above it in a suspended state, or in a partial-wetting configuration, indicating different nucleation and spreading modes.\n\nIn the departure stage, the figure contrasts several ways the fluid body leaves the surface. Applied body or surface forces can drive removal over a liquid film or as sliding droplets/bubbles. Coalescence can also trigger ejection, and a final sketch shows direct lift-off when density differences create an upward body force. Overall, the image compares the main mechanisms controlling attachment, enlargement, motion, and release at interfaces.",
